<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · Love song · 1837 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Die Lorelei</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A German song from 1837 about a maiden on a rock above the Rhine whose singing makes boatmen forget to watch the river.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Heinrich Heine</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Friedrich Silcher</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>German Romantic poetry and song</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Germany (its subject is the Lorelei rock on the Rhine, at Sankt Goarshausen)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem 1824; Silcher's setting 1837</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad · Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Heinrich Heine (1824; he died in 1856))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The Lorelei is a steep slate rock, 132 metres high, on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the Rhine Gorge at Sankt Goarshausen. Its strong currents and loud echo made it a place of shipwrecks from the 10th century on, and its name is usually read as &quot;murmuring rock&quot;. Old tales placed dwarfs in caves inside it.</p><p>The woman of the rock was the invention of a poet. In 1801 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clemens-brentano">Clemens Brentano</a> wrote the ballad &quot;Zu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacharach">Bacharach</a> am Rheine&quot;, part of a fragmentary continuation of his novel Godwi. In it the beautiful Lore Lay, betrayed by her sweetheart and accused of bewitching men, is sent to a nunnery instead of being sentenced to death; on the way she climbs the rock to look at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> once more and falls to her death, and the rock keeps the echo of her name. Brentano drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> and the myth of Echo. Joseph von Eichendorff also used the figure in his poem &quot;Waldesgespräch&quot; (1812), later set by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-schumann">Robert Schumann</a>.</p><p>In 1824 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-heine">Heinrich Heine</a> took up Brentano's theme in &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorelei">Die Lorelei</a>&quot;, one of his most famous poems. His Lorelei is a kind of siren who sits on the cliff combing her golden hair and, without meaning to, distracts the boatmen with her beauty and song, so that they are wrecked on the rocks. In 1837 Friedrich Silcher set Heine's words to music as the art song &quot;Lorelei&quot;, which became well known throughout the German-speaking lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a>'s setting was also popular, and dozens of other musicians set the poem.</p><p>Through the Heine–Silcher song the figure passed into German popular culture, and it is often wrongly believed to come from an old folk tale. Under the Nazi regime and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, Heine, who was born a Jew, was discredited as the author of the words, as part of an effort to hide the Jewish contribution to German art.</p><p>The theme was taken up again by Guillaume Apollinaire in &quot;La Loreley&quot;, in his collection Alcools, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitri-shostakovich">Dmitri Shostakovich</a> used in the third movement of his Symphony No. 14, and it recurs in popular songs from Townes Van Zandt (1969) to The Pogues (1989).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A ballad of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>: a beautiful maiden sits on the high rock combing her golden hair and singing, and a boatman, lost in her beauty and her song, is wrecked on the rocks below. The mood is dreamy and melancholy rather than frightening.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ich weiß nicht, was soll es bedeuten,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I do not know what it should mean</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Daß ich so traurig bin;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That I am so sad;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ein Mährchen aus alten Zeiten,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A tale from olden times</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Das kommt mir nicht aus dem Sinn.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Will not leave my mind.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Luft ist kühl und es dunkelt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The air is cool and it grows dark,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und ruhig fließt der Rhein;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And calmly flows the Rhine;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Der Gipfel des Berges funkelt</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The summit of the mountain sparkles</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Im Abendsonnenschein.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the evening sunshine.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die schönste Jungfrau sitzet</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The most beautiful maiden sits</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Dort oben wunderbar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Up there, wondrously;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ihr gold’nes Geschmeide blitzet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Her golden jewellery gleams,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Sie kämmt ihr gold’nes Haar.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She combs her golden hair.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Sie kämmt es mit gold’nem Kamme,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She combs it with a golden comb,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und singt ein Lied dabei;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And sings a song as she does;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Das hat eine wundersame,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It has a wondrous,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Gewaltige Melodei.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mighty melody.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Den Schiffer im kleinen Schiffe</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The boatman in his little boat</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ergreift es mit wildem Weh;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Is seized by it with wild woe;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Er schaut nicht die Felsenriffe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He does not see the rocky reefs,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Er schaut nur hinauf in die Höh’.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He looks only up into the height.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ich glaube, die Wellen verschlingen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I believe the waves swallow</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Am Ende Schiffer und Kahn;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the end boatman and boat;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und das hat mit ihrem Singen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And that, with her singing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Lore-Ley gethan.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Lore-Ley has done.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Heinrich Heine, Buch der Lieder (1827; written 1824). Text from <a href="https://de.wikisource.org/wiki/Ich_wei%C3%9F_nicht%2C_was_soll_es_bedeuten" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>line 1.3: 'Mährchen' is the old spelling of 'Märchen': a tale, legend or fairy tale.</li><li>line 3.2: 'wunderbar' can describe how she sits (wondrously, marvellously) or the maiden herself (wonderful); the word order allows both.</li><li>line 4.4: 'Melodei' is an old, poetic form of 'Melodie', chosen for the rhyme with 'dabei'.</li><li>line 5.1-2: literally 'The boatman in the small boat / it seizes with wild woe': 'es' is the song (or her singing), the subject; the boatman is the object. 'Weh' is pain, woe or longing.</li><li>line 6.1: 'verschlingen' is present tense, as in the German ('I believe the waves swallow'), with the sense 'swallowed up' or 'will swallow up'.</li><li>line 6.2: 'Kahn' is a small boat or skiff, a second word for the 'kleines Schiff' of 5.1.</li><li>line 6.4: 'Lore-Ley' is the name of the rock above the Rhine and of the maiden Heine places on it; kept as a name. 'gethan' is the old spelling of 'getan' (done).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
